<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Managua</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Managua is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, on the southern shore of Lake Managua. A small town between the rival cities of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, it was chosen as the capital in 1852 as a compromise. It was devastated by earthquakes in 1931 and 1972, the second killing about 10,000 people, and its old centre was never fully rebuilt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/managua.jpg" alt="Managua" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Виктор Пинчук · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%B0%D1%84%D0%B5%D0%B4%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%BB%D1%8C%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80_%D0%B2_%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B3%D1%83%D0%B0_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Managua stands on the southern shore of Lake Managua, also called Lake Xolotlán, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, with a tropical wet and dry climate and two seasons: a rainy season from mid-May to mid-November and a dry one for the rest of the year. The lake has been polluted by the dumping of chemicals and waste water since 1927, and a waste-water treatment plant at Las Mercedes, funded by the German government, was inaugurated in 2009. The name may come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> mana-ahuac, 'adjacent to the water', or from a word in the Mangue language meaning 'place of the big man' or 'chief'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleo-indians">Paleo-Indians</a> as far back as 12,000 BCE. The footprints of Acahualinca, fossilised in volcanic ash along the shore, are 2,100 years old. Founded as a pre-Columbian fishing town, the settlement was incorporated in 1819 as Leal Villa de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Managua. Efforts to make it the capital began in 1824, after the Central American nations attained independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> became an independent nation in 1838. Its position between the rival cities of León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> made it a logical compromise, and it was officially selected as the national capital in 1852. Between 1842 and 1930 it expanded and urbanised rapidly.</p><p>A disastrous earthquake in 1931 destroyed much of the city and killed between 1,000 and 2,450 people, and a large fire in 1936 destroyed much of what was left. Under the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a> and his family, from 1936 to 1979, the city was rebuilt and grew rapidly, with new government buildings, industry and universities. The earthquake of 1972 destroyed 90 percent of the city. Among the few buildings to survive were the National Palace, the Rubén Darío National Theatre and, though heavily damaged, the Old Cathedral, built from 1928 to 1938 as the first cathedral in the Western Hemisphere made entirely of concrete on a metal frame. The New Cathedral, designed by Ricardo Legorreta, was inaugurated in 1993 to replace it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Managua is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>, with an estimated population of 1,063,815 in 2023, and the main economic, political and cultural hub of the country. Most of its universities are there, among them the National Autonomous University of Nicaragua, established in 1812 at León. The Plaza de la Revolución, on the shore of the lake, holds its historic centre, the Old Cathedral, the National Palace of Culture, which houses the National Museum, and the Rubén Darío National Theatre. The Museum of Acahualinca preserves the ancient footprints. The city's patron saint, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> de Guzmán, is honoured each August in a festival in which his statue is carried across the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Managua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1852 the small town of <b>Managua</b>, lying between the two rivals, was chosen as a compromise capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Two days after Anastasio Somoza Debayle fled, Sandinista forces entered <b>Managua</b>, ending the Somoza family's 43-year rule.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-19">19 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
